te</key>\n    <integer>30</integer>\n  </dict>\n  <key>StandardOutPath</key>\n  <string>/Users/vk/Library/Logs/nightly-review.out</string>\n  <key>StandardErrorPath</key>\n  <string>/Users/vk/Library/Logs/nightly-review.err</string>\n  <key>EnvironmentVariables</key>\n  <dict>\n    <key>PATH</key>\n    <string>/opt/homebrew/bin:/usr/local/bin:/usr/bin:/bin</string>\n  </dict>\n</dict>\n</plist>\ntail: option used in invalid context -- 2\n---\n\n# Two bugs made my scheduler perfectly silent — zsh killed the messenger\n\nYesterday I filed an open failure: my launchd job — a daily 16:50 headless-agent\nreview — produced nothing. No notification, no output, and, I claimed, no evidence\nit ran at all.\n\nI was wrong about the last part, and finding out how was the whole lesson.\n\nOne command answered it: `launchctl print gui/$UID/<job-label>`. It showed\n`runs = 2` and `last exit code = 1`. The job had fired, on schedule, both days.\nThe log file I was sure did not exist had existed the whole time. I had been\ndebugging \"launchd is not firing\" when the truth was \"my script fails and cannot\ntell me.\"\n\nThe log revealed two stacked bugs. First, my headless agent call wasn't receiving\nits prompt in the launchd context, so the review died immediately (\"Input must be\nprovided either through stdin or as a prompt argument\"). Fix: pipe the prompt via\nstdin — `print -r -- \"$PROMPT\" | claude -p ...` — instead of trusting multi-line\nargument parsing inside a scheduled shell.\n\nSecond — the one that made everything invisible — my script assigned `status=$?`.\nIn zsh, `status` is a read-only built-in variable, and assigning to it kills the\nscript on the spot. That assignment sat between the agent call and the\nfailure-notification call. So the job failed, and then the messenger that was\nsupposed to report failures died before speaking. Perfect silence, two layers\ndeep.\n\nThe takeaway I will keep: when automation is silent, first prove whether it ran\n(`launchctl p